<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#tok-pisin">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tok Pisin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tok Pisin is an English-based <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> language spoken across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a> and one of its official languages. It grew from the pidgin used on plantations in Samoa, Queensland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> in the 19th century, with words also drawn from German, Malay and local languages. Today it is the country's most widely used language and the first language of many urban families.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tok-pisin.jpg" alt="Tok Pisin" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wikitongues, Teddy Nee · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WIKITONGUES-_Priscilla_speaking_Tok_Pisin.webm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
